---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias30.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Income Statement Disclosures" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Maturity Analysis of Assets and Liabilities" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Concentrations of Assets, Liabilities, and Off-Balance-Sheet Items" },
        { id: "s6", label: "The Hidden Reserves Prohibition" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Losses on Loans and Advances" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Why IAS 30 Was Replaced" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Summary" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IAS 30 vs Modern IFRS 7" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="withdrawn"
    explanation="IAS 30 (issued 1990) provided disclosure requirements specific to banks and similar financial institutions, including maturity analyses, concentrations of risk, and the hidden reserves prohibition. It was superseded by IFRS 7 Financial Instruments: Disclosures (2007), which extended similar (and in many respects more rigorous) disclosure principles to all entities holding financial instruments, not just banks specifically."
    historical="It is retained here for historical reference. For current financial instrument disclosure requirements, always apply IFRS 7."
    link="/ifrs/ifrs7"
    linkText="IFRS 7 Financial Instruments: Disclosures"
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why banks needed industry-specific disclosure guidance</div>
        <div class="principle">Banks and similar institutions face risk profiles (liquidity, concentration, credit) that are both more pronounced and structurally different from ordinary commercial entities — a bank's core business is managing financial risk. The Conceptual Framework's relevance objective demanded disclosure requirements tailored to this reality, which IAS 30 provided as a dedicated, industry-specific disclosure standard.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 30 MAPPED TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Relevance</strong> — maturity and concentration disclosures gave users insight into a bank's genuine liquidity and credit risk exposure, which ordinary financial statements alone would not adequately convey</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Faithful representation</strong> — the explicit prohibition on "hidden reserves" (undisclosed provisions used to smooth reported profit) directly enforced transparent, faithful reporting of a bank's true financial position</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Industry-specific vs universal disclosure</strong> — IAS 30's bank-specific scope reflected an earlier standard-setting philosophy of addressing certain risks through dedicated, industry-focused standards, rather than universal principles applicable to all entities</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 30's eventual replacement by the universally applicable IFRS 7 (covering all entities' financial instrument disclosures, not just banks) reflects the IASB's later preference for broadly applicable, principles-based standards over narrow industry carve-outs — a recurring pattern across many withdrawn standards in this library.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Key disclosure areas IAS 30 pioneered</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Disclosure area</span><span>Purpose</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Maturity analysis</span><span>Liquidity risk transparency</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Concentrations of risk</span><span>Credit/geographic/industry risk transparency</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Hidden reserves prohibition</span><span>Preventing profit-smoothing via undisclosed provisions</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;">These three disclosure pillars are direct ancestors of IFRS 7's liquidity risk maturity analysis, credit risk concentration disclosure, and general transparency requirements — applied today to all entities, not just banks.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 30 covered</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 30 applied to the financial statements of banks and similar financial institutions (including some other institutions accepting deposits and dealing in money and monetary instruments) — a narrowly targeted, industry-specific scope.</div>
        <div class="note">This narrow, industry-specific scope is the key structural difference from IFRS 7, which applies universally regardless of industry.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know (historical)</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Bank (original, broad concept)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An institution whose principal activities include taking deposits and borrowing with the object of lending and investing, and that is included within banking legislation in the jurisdiction of operation.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Hidden reserve</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A reserve that understates assets, or overstates liabilities, resulting in an understatement of net assets not apparent from the financial statements.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Income Statement Disclosures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Broad categories of interest income/expense and fee income</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Banks disclosed major categories of interest and similar income, interest expense and similar charges, and fee/commission income and expense — providing users with a clear picture of the fundamental sources of a bank's core operating revenue, distinct from ordinary commercial entities' revenue disclosure conventions.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Maturity Analysis of Assets and Liabilities (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The direct ancestor of IFRS 7's liquidity risk disclosure</div>
        <div class="principle">Banks disclosed an analysis of assets and liabilities into relevant maturity groupings, based on the remaining period to the contractual maturity date — providing users with insight into a bank's liquidity position (whether assets and liabilities' maturities were reasonably matched).</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: A simplified maturity grouping disclosure</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A bank discloses loans and advances of $500,000,000 maturing within 1 year, and customer deposits of $650,000,000 also maturing (or repayable on demand) within 1 year.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">This disclosure reveals a potential liquidity mismatch — deposits repayable within a year exceed loans maturing in the same period by $150,000,000, meaning the bank must either roll over deposits or source alternative funding to meet obligations — precisely the kind of insight IAS 30's maturity analysis was designed to surface, and the direct conceptual ancestor of IFRS 7's own liquidity risk maturity analysis requirement today.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Concentrations of Assets, Liabilities, and Off-Balance-Sheet Items</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Surfacing concentrated risk exposures</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Banks disclosed significant concentrations of assets, liabilities, and off-balance-sheet items — by geographical area, customer/industry group, or other risk concentration — helping users understand where a bank's risk was disproportionately exposed to a single counterparty, sector, or region, a direct forerunner of IFRS 7's own credit risk concentration disclosure requirement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Hidden Reserves Prohibition (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A uniquely bank-focused transparency safeguard</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 30 explicitly prohibited the historical banking practice of maintaining "hidden reserves" — undisclosed reserves used to smooth reported profit across periods by understating assets or overstating provisions in good years, then releasing them in bad years, without transparent disclosure. Banks were required to disclose the aggregate amount of any general banking risk provisions, and movements in them, preventing this opaque practice.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Hidden reserve manipulation IAS 30 prohibited</div>
          <div class="wk-body">In a historically strong profit year, a bank sets aside an excessive, undisclosed general provision of $50,000,000 (beyond what specific credit risk actually warrants), intending to release it quietly in a future weaker year to smooth reported earnings.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">IAS 30 required disclosure of the aggregate amount of such general provisions and their movements — preventing this kind of opaque earnings-smoothing practice, since users could see the provision was created and could scrutinise its justification, rather than it remaining hidden within undisclosed reserves.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Losses on Loans and Advances</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">An early ancestor of today's credit risk/ECL disclosures</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Banks disclosed the accounting policy for recognising losses on loans/advances, the movement in any provision for losses during the period, and the aggregate amount of the provision at the reporting date — a considerably simpler disclosure regime than IFRS 9's later three-stage expected credit loss model and IFRS 7's detailed staging disclosures, but addressing fundamentally the same underlying user need.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Why IAS 30 Was Replaced</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Universal application replacing an industry-specific carve-out</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">As the IASB developed a comprehensive financial instrument disclosure standard (IFRS 7) applicable to all entities — recognising that liquidity, credit, and market risk disclosures were valuable for many types of entities, not just banks — it made sense to withdraw the narrower, bank-specific IAS 30 and fold its principles into the universal IFRS 7, which offers equivalent (and in several respects more rigorous, e.g. sensitivity analysis, fair value hierarchy) disclosure requirements applicable across the entire economy.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Summary (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures under IAS 30</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Major categories of interest and fee income/expense</span>
          <span>Maturity analysis of assets and liabilities</span>
          <span>Significant concentrations of assets, liabilities, and off-balance-sheet items</span>
          <span>Aggregate amount of general banking risk provisions and movements, prohibiting undisclosed "hidden reserves"</span>
          <span>Losses on loans/advances — accounting policy, provision movements, and aggregate balance</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement mattered most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Identifying significant concentrations warranting separate disclosure</span>
              <span>Determining appropriate maturity groupings for the analysis</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Provisions for losses on loans/advances</span>
              <span>General banking risk provision amounts, subject to the transparency requirement</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">Prior to IAS 30's "hidden reserves" prohibition, historical banking practice in some jurisdictions permitted genuinely opaque profit smoothing — a practice this standard was specifically designed to eliminate through mandatory transparency.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IAS 30 vs Modern IFRS 7</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IAS 30 (1990) vs current IFRS 7</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 30 (withdrawn)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">Current (IFRS 7)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Applicable entities</span><span>Banks and similar financial institutions only</span><span>All entities holding financial instruments, regardless of industry</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Maturity analysis</span><span>Contractual maturity groupings for assets/liabilities</span><span>Undiscounted contractual cash flow maturity analysis for financial liabilities specifically, more precisely defined</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Concentration disclosure</span><span>Geographic/customer/industry concentrations</span><span>Similar credit risk concentration disclosure, now framed within a broader risk management disclosure framework</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Sensitivity analysis / fair value hierarchy</span><span>Not addressed</span><span>Mandatory market risk sensitivity analysis and three-level fair value hierarchy disclosure</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS 7 represents a significant expansion beyond IAS 30's bank-specific content — extending similar principles universally, while also adding entirely new disclosure dimensions (sensitivity analysis, fair value hierarchy) that IAS 30 never contemplated.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping (Historical, Under IAS 30)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Interest/fee income and expense categories</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Ancestor of IFRS 7's own interest income disclosure</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Maturity analysis</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Ancestor of IFRS 7's liquidity risk maturity analysis</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Concentrations of assets/liabilities</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Ancestor of IFRS 7's credit risk concentration disclosure</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>General banking risk provision movements</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory transparency, preventing hidden reserves</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Loan/advance loss provisions</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Recognised per the bank's accounting policy, disclosed separately</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 7</span><span>The direct successor, extending similar (and expanded) disclosures universally</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 9</span><span>Now governs the recognition/measurement/impairment of the loans and financial instruments IAS 30's disclosures related to</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 37</span><span>Governs general provisions recognition, complementing IAS 30's historical transparency requirement around such provisions</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Hidden reserve", definition: "An undisclosed reserve understating assets or overstating liabilities, historically used by some banks to smooth reported profit — expressly prohibited by IAS 30." },
      { term: "General banking risk provision", definition: "A provision for risks generally inherent in banking business, distinct from specific identified loan losses — subject to mandatory transparency under IAS 30." },
      { term: "Maturity analysis", definition: "A breakdown of assets and liabilities into time bands based on their contractual maturity dates, providing users with insight into a bank's liquidity risk profile." },
      { term: "Concentration of risk", definition: "A significant exposure to a single counterparty, industry, or geographical area that could result in disproportionate losses if adverse events occur." },
      { term: "Off-balance-sheet item", definition: "A financial instrument or arrangement that is not recorded on the balance sheet but that creates or modifies risks for the bank, such as guarantees, commitments, and derivatives." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
